 last cache  process and really this is kind of like  something that you're pretty much always  going to battle  as long if you have like a web  application so as the web application  grows and even as your website grows  you're gonna constantly be trying to  make it as efficient as possible while  at the same time retaining you know all  the good stuff that you wanted to keep  right I like to think that Google has it  in mind that depending on what's  actually being loaded here is somewhat  important right if you're just loading a  bunch of static HTML and you've got a  really long loading time then you've  probably got really poor hosting in  relation to how many people are visiting  your website and so I think that  probably is gonna rank more negatively  than if you've got like maybe some sort  of game or something that's just gonna  take a little bit to load right and then  time on site you know they'll probably  take that into consideration as well  like how long was the loading time  versus how much time are they spending  on that page I imagine is important to  them and along those same lines when it  comes to loading time is you know what  kind of hosting do you have for your  website so if you have shared hosting  for example you're usually sharing a  server with somebody else and so if they  get a lot of visitors at one time and  you get a lot of visitors at one time  all of a sudden you can get it  overloaded we run out of RAM go into  like swap memory and your website is  just almost grinding to a halt all right  and so that can be pretty bad so so if  as your website's traffic increases you  want to also obviously increase your  hosting because it will affect that will  affect your loading time and the user  experience as well and it's usually a  good idea to upgrade before you need to  upgrade right  I mean web hosting is pretty darn cheap  these days so it kind of moves you to go  ahead and stay a little bit ahead of  where you are at the moment now 